# KNOWN GAP — MEMORY UNSAFE, BOTH BACKENDS, from ten lines of ordinary safe
# Gorget. A `Callable` bound to a LOCAL, whose signature takes a STRUCT-typed
# `&` param, SEGFAULTS when called.
#
# ⚠ THE DISCRIMINATOR IS A COMBINATION — measured as a 2x2 on 2026-08-19, and
# it is NARROWER than any of the three entries filed against this family:
#
#   binding | param type | result
#   --------|------------|--------------------------------
#   LOCAL   | int &      | OK, prints 2
#   LOCAL   | struct &   | SIGSEGV rc 139 on C AND LLVM   <-- this fixture
#   PARAM   | struct &   | OK, prints 2
#   (direct call, no Callable, struct &)  OK, prints 2
#
# So it is NOT "indirect calls are broken" (a param-passed Callable with the
# same struct `&` signature works), and NOT "struct `&` params are broken"
# (the direct call and the param form both work). It is the LOCAL binding
# combined with a struct-typed `&` parameter.
#
# ⚠ WHY THE EXISTING FIXTURE DOES NOT CATCH IT: `tests/fixtures/callable_ref_param.gg`
# samples `Callable[int(Counter &)]` — a struct `&` param — and PASSES, because
# it passes the callable as a PARAMETER. It covers the adjacent WORKING cell of
# the same 2x2. Core #12: a green cell may be green for a reason unrelated to
# what you think it tests, and a fixture set that samples one value of a typed
# axis is an anecdote.
#
# Measured 2026-08-19 at HEAD:
#     gg check                -> "OK: no semantic errors"  (rc 0)
#     gg build (C)            -> rc 0, then the binary exits 139 (SIGSEGV)
#     gg build --backend=llvm -> rc 0, then the binary exits 139 (SIGSEGV)
#
# INTENDED: prints `2` on both backends — the callee writes through the `&`.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_callable_local_struct_amp_param_segv (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d). Un-ignore when the local-bound callable carries the same `&`
# calling convention the parameter-passed form already gets.
struct P:
    int n

void bump(P &p):
    p.n = p.n + 1

int main():
    Callable[void(P &)] f = bump
    P p = P(1)
    f(&p)
    print(p.n)
    return 0
